A third release run with per-station position checks passed (600 frames,
66,188 ns mean, 3,130,330 ns maximum, zero misses). Fresh validation
passed in run 1795593147156550715-1327130.

A paired manifest migration diagnostic on 2026-11-25 used the same native
Linux debug executable, adaptive 400x304 logical scene, touch controls,
Xvfb, software OpenGL and dummy audio. The legacy and version-two
catalogues referenced 67 equivalent bundles; all 292 payload files
(23,331,508 bytes) matched byte for byte. Both catalogues verified 80
added scene columns and 80 street-camera columns. Two interleaved trials
per catalogue measured the existing 600-frame four-player movement slice:

- Legacy: mean 1.331–1.398 ms, maximum 9.604 ms, p95 ranges spanning
  1.573–1.966 ms, peak process RSS 124,020–124,232 KiB.
- Version two: mean 1.249–1.263 ms, maximum 7.363 ms, p95 ranges spanning
  1.311–1.704 ms, peak process RSS 123,632–124,152 KiB.
- All 2,400 measured frames met the 16.667 ms target. Whole-process wall
  time was 1.51–1.57 s for legacy and 1.41–1.45 s for version two.
  The unstripped debug executable occupied 49,047,392 bytes.

GNU time measured the process RSS and whole-process wall time separately
from the game's frame collector. These two samples per catalogue do not
establish a statistically significant speedup, isolated asset load time,
physical GPU performance, display latency or release binary size.

The native diagnostics additionally report the kernel's approximate
process-lifetime resident high-water mark (VmHWM), separately from the
maximum sampled VmRSS. Both probes share strict unit and overflow checks;
missing or invalid kernel fields remain unobserved. Tests distinguish
current, lifetime, zero and absent measurements, and exercise real procfs.

Two release trials with 600 frames of the verified 1,024-draw 256x256
atlas workload under Xvfb and software OpenGL had zero 60 Hz misses.
Mean host completion intervals were 401,290–404,390 ns; sampled peak
RSS was 168,910,848–169,263,104 bytes, and kernel VmHWM was
166,948,864–168,476,672 bytes. The executable occupied 1,599,760 bytes.
The lower lifetime readings demonstrate that these approximate kernel
counters must not be treated as exact or interchangeable. VmHWM includes
earlier process activity; neither probe measures VRAM or a precise
benchmark-only memory peak.

The hidden title smoke now submits all 17 fixed-tick poses of each of
