The interactive arcade host now waits at most eight milliseconds for an
event on suspended screens. Fixed updates, transport polling and audio
maintenance continue; the host redraws on native activity, cabinet or
remote-seat changes, and retries unsuccessful presentation. Gameplay
continues using uncapped presentation. An inline regression exercises
initial drawing, unchanged screens, event batches, remote admission
and disconnect, and entry into and exit from active gameplay.

A paired release-mode interactive diagnostic used the same verified
version-two catalogue, Xvfb, software OpenGL and dummy audio. Linux
process CPU utilisation (100% per logical processor) was sampled using
four one-second top intervals after an initial warm-up sample:

- Main menu: baseline 847.0–899.1%, revised 0–1%.
- Paused: baseline 795.0–897.0%, revised 0–1%.

Both builds preserved exact pixels over four seconds on each static
screen; the initial main menu matched across builds pixel for pixel.
Both accepted keyboard entry, pause, window resize to 1080x756 and
menu-driven exit. The revised active game still rendered uncapped.
These are single paired software-rendered process observations, not
physical GPU utilisation or input-to-photon measurements. The fresh
workspace validation passed in run 1795605886548083151-4173262.

The optional native `--measure` launch flag instruments visible gameplay
without changing the fixed tick or presentation policy. It times host
loop work through successful submission, samples RSS every 120 drawn
frames, and records one oldest applied local SDL event age per frame.
Only nonempty admitted action batches enter the timing ledger; rejected
batches retain their original timestamp through retry. Execution of
their actual target tick makes them eligible for a subsequent successful
gameplay submission. Remote input, physical scanout and GPU completion
are not measured. Pausing or changing logical extent or display settings
separates reports; skipped submissions do not create frame samples.

A visible Xvfb/software-OpenGL run with the verified local version-two
art and four-seat X11 keyboard workload submitted 1,918 aspect-fit
320x224 frames: mean host work 1.19 ms, one miss of 16.67 ms, 35
applied-input age samples with mean 10.6 ms, and sampled peak RSS
118,550,528 bytes. A second run resized the adaptive window and recorded
three separate 320x224, 400x224 and 320x224 segments (254, 1,551 and
1,028 submitted frames respectively), with no 16.67 ms misses.
The 400x224 segment averaged 1.40 ms host work and 10.47 ms age for
30 applied-input samples. Both runs produced visibly different
before/after captures and exited through the keyboard-driven menu.
The figures describe these local software-rendered sessions, not
physical hardware, display latency or end-to-end LAN latency.
